{"name":"Billabong","hex":"#1b6f81","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Billabong","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A locality in the Shire of Wellington, south eastern Victoria, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Billabong"}],"entries":[{"title":"Billabong","blocks":[{"text":"In Australian English, a billabong is a small body of water, usually a permanent one created by a change in course or the flooding of a river. It is variously used to refer to oxbow lakes, dry creek beds that fill after heavy rainfall and channels of rivers that lead to dead-ends or backwaters. The term is likely borrowed from Wiradjuri, an Aboriginal Australian language of New South Wales."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Berrinba_Wetlands_billabong.jpg/330px-Berrinba_Wetlands_billabong.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billabong","wikidata":"Q803749"},{"title":"Billabong (clothing)","blocks":[{"text":"Billabong International Limited is an Australian company focused on surfing, primarily a clothing retailer that also produces accessories, such as watches and backpacks, and skateboard and snowboard products under other brand names. Founded in 1973 by Gordon and Rena Merchant, the company first traded on the Australian Securities Exchange in August 2000. The name \"billabong\" is derived from the Wiradjuri word bilabaŋ that refers to a \"creek that runs only during the rainy season\". As of September 2013, Von Zipper and Element were two of the prominent brands that Billabong owns. Honolua Surf Company, Kustom, Palmers Surf, Xcel, Tigerlily, Sector 9, and RVCA were the company's other brands.\nIn 2018, Billabong International Limited was acquired by Boardriders, Inc, owner of rival brand Quiksilver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c8/Billabong_logo.svg/330px-Billabong_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billabong_(clothing)","wikidata":"Q862718"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Billabong_-FWS.jpg/500px-Billabong_-FWS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Billabong_-FWS.jpg/500px-Billabong_-FWS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A billabong in the Northern Territory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABillabong_-FWS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg/500px-Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg/1280px-Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg/500px-Billabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A billabong on the Goulburn River in Victoria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABillabong_at_Tahbilk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Billabong_-_Junction_32_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1166758.jpg/500px-Billabong_-_Junction_32_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1166758.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Billabong_-_Junction_32_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1166758.jpg/500px-Billabong_-_Junction_32_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1166758.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A shop in Glasshoughton, West Yorkshire, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABillabong_-_Junction_32_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1166758.j